        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities: Mike's original typing speed (65 words per minute), his reduced typing speed (45 words per minute due to the accident), and the total number of words in the document (810 words). The unknown I needed to find was the time it would take Mike to type the document at his reduced speed.\n\nTo solve this, I used the formula for time, which is the total number of words divided by the words per minute. This formula is based on the direct proportionality between the number of words and the time taken to type them. I calculated the time as follows:\n\\[ \\text{Time} = \\frac{810 \\text{ words}}{45 \\text{ words per minute}} = 18 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nI expected this result to mean that it would take Mike 18 minutes to type the document at his reduced speed. To check this, I considered that if Mike types 45 words per minute, in 18 minutes he would type:\n\\[ 45 \\text{ words per minute} \\times 18 \\text{ minutes} = 810 \\text{ words} \\]\nThis matches the total number of words in the document, confirming my calculation.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is the formula for calculating time based on the total number of words and the typing speed. This operation is reusable in any scenario where you need to determine the time required to complete a task given the total amount of work and the rate of work.\n\nFINAL: 18",
