        "target": "Given the problem, I identified the key quantities and the unknown:\n- Jerry's one-way trip time to school: 15 minutes\n- Distance to school: 4 miles\n- Carson's one-way trip time to school: 30 minutes (since it's the same as Jerry's round trip time)\n\nI first converted Jerry's one-way trip time to hours to facilitate the calculation:\n- 15 minutes = 0.25 hours\n\nSince Jerry's round trip time is 30 minutes (0.5 hours), and this is equal to Carson's one-way trip time, I calculated Carson's speed using the formula:\n- Speed = Distance / Time\n- Carson's speed = 4 miles / 0.5 hours = 8 miles per hour\n\nTo verify this, I checked the consistency of the units and the arithmetic:\n- Distance: 4 miles\n- Time: 0.5 hours\n- Speed: 8 miles per hour\n\nThe calculation is straightforward and the units are consistent, confirming that Carson's speed is indeed 8 miles per hour.\n\nThe operation I would reuse is the formula for speed (Speed = Distance / Time) whenever I need to find the speed given the distance and time. This operation is reusable in any scenario where speed needs to be calculated from known distance and time.\n\nFINAL: 8",
