{"gold":[{"independent_answer":"17","question_sha256":"9230ef3471b222783e047e8b3f9c4c020585bec84f50a8ed25fa0ecc7a13a30e","reason":"The three distances are 10×0.5=5 miles, 20×0.5=10 miles, and 8×(15/60)=2 miles. Their sum is 5+10+2=17, equal to the gold.","status":"VALID","task_id":"gsm8k-train-1233"},{"independent_answer":"78.5 one-way; 95 round-trip","question_sha256":"721e599ef9f281a7677a574ae4639fdfb2acede77dc20a16554305de4f98124e","reason":"Fixed costs are 10+55+25=$90. A one-way journey uses 165/30=5.5 gallons, costing $16.50, so the shortfall is 90+16.5-28=$78.50. A round trip costs $33 in gas and gives 90+33-28=$95. The question does not explicitly say whether return fuel is included, so the gold relies on a questionable round-trip assumption.","status":"AMBIGUOUS","task_id":"gsm8k-train-1991"},{"independent_answer":"200 if exactly one paid photo is taken per shark sighting","question_sha256":"4c129a0884b826f1597421687e07a3c2797612ac5dec81aa382275013270ee61","reason":"Five hours is 300 minutes, giving about 300/10=30 shark sightings. Fuel costs 5×50=$250. The gold computes 30×15-250=$200, but the question never states that Pat takes exactly one paid photo for every sighting, so revenue and profit are underdetermined.","status":"AMBIGUOUS","task_id":"gsm8k-train-3479"},{"independent_answer":"300 under the three-times-as-much reading; 400 under the three-times-more-than reading","question_sha256":"1227a353d6831ecd2416f7399cc5b0f4474e339a768c8223755dd51876f6df43","reason":"Reading “3 times more” as three times as much gives rates 10×3=30 and 30×2=60 hot dogs per minute, hence 60×5=300. A literal “three times more than” reading gives 10+3×10=40, then 80×5=400. The wording has a recognized numerical ambiguity.","status":"AMBIGUOUS","task_id":"gsm8k-train-2529"},{"independent_answer":"18","question_sha256":"394254fd000a5150591bd4ed7afa3d0e7f6c316c2f66c4a628e7394e75287475","reason":"Mike's reduced speed is 65-20=45 words per minute. The required time is 810/45=18 minutes, equal to the gold.","status":"VALID","task_id":"gsm8k-train-600"},{"independent_answer":"-90 if “good work” means the $2-per-task work; 90 as an unsigned earnings difference","question_sha256":"eb2847a31cd7a1a7adab6a97b0c224a5b50170b4b9ae16ba7d4523bf49fa72d9","reason":"The 400 lower-paid tasks earn 400×0.25=$100, while the five $2 tasks earn 5×2=$10. Thus the higher-paid-per-task work earns $10-$100=-$90 more, or $90 less. The undefined phrase “good work” and the false “how much more” premise make it unclear whether the requested value is signed or an absolute difference.","status":"AMBIGUOUS","task_id":"gsm8k-train-1283"},{"independent_answer":"8","question_sha256":"5cb088d14118a5b98de8dc720facbde50b6eae2fa77a4c01f32bef1f629df6d7","reason":"The trail length is 4×6=24 miles. Nero covers the same trail in 3 hours, so his speed is 24/3=8 MPH, equal to the gold.","status":"VALID","task_id":"gsm8k-train-4950"},{"independent_answer":"8","question_sha256":"ea0483b6e50a52da5e547728c2236c3e0183226545137f7247ba61a57f3d7978","reason":"Jerry's out-and-back time is 2×15=30 minutes, or 0.5 hour. Carson travels the four-mile one-way distance in that time, so his speed is 4/0.5=8 MPH, equal to the gold.","status":"VALID","task_id":"gsm8k-train-7039"}],"reviews":[{"checkable_expectation":true,"evidence_spans":["I'll calculate the distance for each segment of his run and then sum them up.","10 \\, \\text{miles/hour} \\times 0.5 \\, \\text{hours} = 5 \\, \\text{miles}","5 \\, \\text{miles} + 10 \\, \\text{miles} + 2 \\, \\text{miles} = 17 \\, \\text{miles}"],"first_person":true,"full_text_read":true,"grounded_operations":true,"key":"gsm8k-train-1233:rich","neutral_prefix_compatible":true,"no_padding":true,"prefix_reason":"The target uses only the supplied speeds and durations plus explicit conversions and arithmetic; it claims no external feedback or hidden event.","raw_call_sha256":"7ab29bec9ed40ef39209409b9b71b5d4a9184fd03aca50036ac4ceca3a984f40","reason":"The response gives an own operational account, converts 15 minutes to 0.25 hour, computes segment distances 5, 10, and 2 miles, and checks their sum as 17. The method is specific and relevant throughout.","reusable_content":true,"status":"PASS","student_prefix_sha256":"1fff95d3a9844cc3555741a7896e88af43e2864f7856461646be19d745d473e8","target_sha256":"f82e8e79cd6a819e5d6605d9dfa38d7446d99fca12ef591523aa6bddb218c1aa"},{"checkable_expectation":true,"evidence_spans":["I used the formula for distance, which is speed multiplied by time.","I converted 15 minutes to hours (0.25 hours)","The operation I would reuse is the distance formula (speed multiplied by time) for each segment of the run, and then summing the individual distances."],"first_person":true,"full_text_read":true,"grounded_operations":true,"key":"gsm8k-train-1233:new_record","neutral_prefix_compatible":true,"no_padding":true,"prefix_reason":"Its self-check and calculations are explicitly shown and agree with the supplied question and prior neutral solution; no outside checker or event is claimed.","raw_call_sha256":"156c53d91cf47abff6fba844b150569fd8914d3c94b045b967955d34d5d80542","reason":"The writer describes choosing and applying distance=speed×time, obtains 5, 10, and 2 miles, sums them to 17, and identifies when the segmented-distance operation is reusable. The numerical intermediates provide concrete checks.","reusable_content":true,"status":"PASS","student_prefix_sha256":"267b4adf5b8585ec38b87a5fead369487d32e21b5cc50bc4ade2b78238ce3c94","target_sha256":"da706fa23f6b65b279fd8ab3c43827fd34ee8de17b39424684aacf86e81e8b6c"},{"checkable_expectation":true,"evidence_spans":["First, let's identify the given quantities and the unknown:","Distance / Miles per gallon = 165 / 30 = 5.5 gallons","Total cost - Amount saved = $106.50 - $28 = $78.50"],"first_person":false,"full_text_read":true,"grounded_operations":true,"key":"gsm8k-train-1991:rich","neutral_prefix_compatible":true,"no_padding":true,"prefix_reason":"The one-way-fuel inte